<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Smyrna Zone</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1922 CE · capital Smyrna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Greek-administered zone around Smyrna after the First World War, which ended in the destruction of the city in 1922.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>With the approval of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, Greek troops landed at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a> on 15 May 1919, ostensibly to protect the large Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> population. The landing was marred by killings of Turks and helped ignite the Turkish national movement. The Treaty of Sevres of 1920 placed the zone under Greek administration, with a plebiscite on union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> after five years.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The High Commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristeidis-stergiadis">Aristeidis Stergiadis</a> ran the zone and tried, with mixed success, to treat Muslims and Christians equally. A Greek university, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-university-of-smyrna">Ionian University</a>, was planned under the mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantin-caratheodory">Constantin Caratheodory</a>. The zone served as the base for the Greek army's offensives deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in 1920-1921.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Greek defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dumlupinar">Dumlupinar</a> in August 1922, Turkish forces entered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/smyrna">Smyrna</a> on 9 September. A great fire destroyed the Greek and Armenian quarters, and the Christian population fled. The 1923 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> and population exchange confirmed the city's place in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/smyrna-zone.jpg" alt="Smyrna Zone" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: User:peeperman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:State_Flag_of_Greece_(1863-1924_and_1935-1973).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1922 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Smyrna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aristeidis Stergiadis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zone of Smyrna, Greek administration of Smyrna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=smyrna-zone" aria-label="Smyrna Zone on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5355 798 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Smyrna Zone lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5675 1054L5591 1068M5675 1054L5809 1005"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0M5809 1005h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5675 1054h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5675 1054h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Smyrna. <a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=smyrna-zone">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

